.TH MODBUS-FAKE-INVERTER 1 "2026-11-05" "pv-suite 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
modbus-fake-inverter \- simulated PV inverter for testing the pv-suite stack
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modbus-fake-inverter
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B modbus-fake-inverter \-\-static
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A stdlib-only Modbus TCP server that plays an inverter \(em with a
battery, a smart meter, the modbus-cache diagnostics block and the
pv-suite's register-map profiles \(em so the scraper, the cache and the
whole stack can be exercised without hardware. The default profile is the
Huawei SUN2000 map; Deye hybrids are included. This is a test instrument:
synthetic data only, no state worth protecting beyond its LAN segment.
.PP
By default it simulates a sunny day: a double-shouldered PV curve over
two MPPT strings (east/west), a home load with morning/evening peaks, a
battery state machine (95 % charge efficiency, self-discharge, an 80 %
discharge cap, temperature derating), per-phase grid voltages and load
shares (mean-reverting wanderers), a grid-frequency machine, and a grid
outage machine (random blackouts). All machines integrate with a simulated
clock \(em UTC by default, freezable (\fB--time\fR) and scalable
(\fB--time-scale\fR for time-lapse). \fB--static\fR replaces the day with
constant demo values. Writes (FC06/FC16) land in an overlay the scrapers
read; \fB--chaos\fR answers a fraction of requests hostile.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-host " HOST" ", " \-\-port " N"
the Modbus TCP listener. Defaults \fB127.0.0.1:5502\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-unit " N"
the unit id served. Default \fB1\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME|PATH"
the register-map profile (a bare name or a JSON path). Default the
bundled \fBsun2000\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-static
constant demo values (PV 4000 W, home 4300 W, grid \-1500 W, battery
+1200 W at 65 % SoC) instead of the day simulation.
.TP
.BI \-\-max-kwp " KW"
PV array size; the peak is 75 % of it. Default \fB6\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-battery-kwh " KWH"
usable battery capacity. Default \fB6.9\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-battery " / " \-\-no-battery
simulate the battery (the default) or not.
.TP
.BI \-\-timezone " utc|local"
the clock the machines integrate over. Default \fButc\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-time " HH:MM"
freeze the simulated clock at this time (zone-independent).
.TP
.BI \-\-time-scale " N"
the simulated clock runs N\(en real time (a full simulated day takes
24/N real hours). Default \fB1\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-grid-voltage " V"
nominal phase voltage, \fB120\fR or \fB230\fR (the per-phase machine
wanders +/-10 %). Default \fB230\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-grid-frequency " HZ"
nominal frequency (the machine wanders +/-0.2 Hz). Default \fB50\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
reject undefined registers with exception 02.
.TP
.BI \-\-max-clients " N"
refuse further Modbus connections past this many simultaneous clients
(\fB0\fR = no limit). Default \fB64\fR. Tunable live on the control
server (\fBset max-clients\fR, \fBshow connections\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-client-timeout " S"
disconnect Modbus clients silent for this many seconds (\fB0\fR = never)
\(em sheds connections from clients that died without closing. Default
\fB300\fR. Tunable live (\fBset client-timeout\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-max-conn-per-ip " N"
refuse further Modbus connections from one peer IP past this many
simultaneous connections (default \fB0\fR = no per-IP limit). Tunable live
(\fBset max-conn-per-ip\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-chaos " P"
probability 0..1 that a request is answered hostile (garbage, short/lying
lengths, wrong ids, stalls, dropouts, raw exceptions) and dropped.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " N"
the chaos/cloud RNG seed for reproducible runs.
.TP
.BI \-\-control-port " TARGET"
the JSON-lines control server: a unix socket path or a TCP port. Default
\fB/tmp/fake-inverter-\fR\fI<modbus-port>\fB.socket\fR; \fB0\fR = off.
.TP
.BI \-\-control-token " TOKEN" ", " \-\-control-token-file " FILE"
require the token auth handshake on control connections (mutually
exclusive). Over the unix socket a trusted peer (root, the daemon's own
user or \fB--control-trust-uids\fR \(em \fBSO_PEERCRED\fR) skips the
handshake. The file must be owner-only (\fB0600\fR); a group/world-accessible
token file refuses to start. A token passed as \fB--control-token\fR is
visible in \fBps\fR / \fB/proc/*/cmdline\fR \(em prefer the file; the flag
form is for quick tests.
.TP
.BI \-\-control-trust-uids " UIDS"
extra peer uids the unix control socket trusts without the token: a
comma-separated list (a local systemd unit speaks without the secret).
.TP
.B \-\-control-log-replies
DEBUG-log the full reply of every control command.
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics-port " N"
serve the fake's own Prometheus \fB/metrics\fR on 127.0.0.1:\fIN\fR. Off
by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics-token " TOKEN" ", " \-\-metrics-token-file " FILE"
require \fBAuthorization: Bearer\fR on the metrics endpoint (mutually
exclusive; the file must be owner-only \fB0600\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics-max-clients " N" ", " \-\-metrics-client-timeout " S" ", " \-\-metrics-max-conn-per-ip " N"
cap concurrent HTTP clients on the metrics endpoint (default \fB64\fR;
overflow answers 503), time out idle ones (default \fB5\fR s; \fB0\fR = the
same built-in cap) and cap concurrent connections from one peer IP
(default \fB0\fR = no per-IP limit).
.TP
.BI \-\-audit-log " FILE|\-"
the audit trail (default
\fB~/.local/state/fake-inverter/audit.log\fR; \fB\-\fR = stdout).
.TP
.BI \-\-audit-max-mb " N ", \-\-audit-daily ", " \-\-audit-files " N", " \-\-no-audit-log"
the file sink's gated rotation (defaults \fB10\fR / off / \fB3\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
validate the configuration, print the effective settings and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-log-level " LEVEL"
default \fBINFO\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-version
print the version.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
re-apply the \fBFAKE_*\fR environment layer (all-or-nothing).
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
reset the scenario: runtime pins, the cloud machine and the write overlay
are dropped.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
graceful shutdown with a session summary.
.SH CONTROL
The control server speaks JSON lines
(\fB{"cmd": "..."}\fR -> \fB{"status": "ok|err|bye", "text": "..."}\fR;
TCP requires the token when configured). Commands: \fBset\fR/\fBunset\fR/
\fBshow\fR/\fBhelp\fR (26 settings: pv, load, batt, grid, grid-voltage,
grid-frequency, grid-outage, soc, mppt, cloud, timezone, time, time-scale,
scenario, chaos, seed, battery, battery-state, strict, static, max-kwp,
battery-kwh, max-clients, client-timeout, register, profile, log-level),
\fBshow status\fR, \fBshow connections\fR (the live Modbus clients: peer,
age, requests), \fBshow settings\fR, \fBshow registers\fR,
\fBshow version\fR, \fBinspect\fR, \fBreset\fR, \fBshutdown\fR,
\fBquit\fR. See
.BR modbus-fake-ctl (1)
and
.BR modbus-fake-cli (1).
.SH SECURITY
The fake is a development/test instrument, not a security-first design
(the audit's F11): the Modbus surface speaks to any peer that can reach
the port \(em no TLS, no auth, no ACL \(em and the metrics endpoint
serves without a token unless one is configured. Run it on a trusted,
LAN-isolated network, next to the tools it is meant to serve.
\fB--max-clients\fR/\fB--client-timeout\fR cap runaway connections and
the read-count gate keeps a malformed frame from assembling an oversized
reply.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FAKE_CHAOS ", " FAKE_MAX_KWP ", " FAKE_PROFILE ", " FAKE_BATTERY ", " FAKE_STRICT ", " FAKE_TIME ", " FAKE_TIME_SCALE ", " FAKE_LOG_LEVEL
the SIGHUP-reloadable layer.
.TP
.B FAKE_CTL
the default control target for the clients.
.TP
.B FAKE_CONTROL_TOKEN ", " FAKE_CLI_KEEPALIVE
the clients' TCP token and idle-ping interval.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/fake-inverter-<port>.socket
the default control socket.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/fake-inverter/audit.log
the default audit trail.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR modbus-fake-ctl (1),
.BR modbus-fake-cli (1),
.BR modbus-cache (1),
.BR modbus-scraper (1)
.PP
\fBinverter/README.md\fR in the suite checkout \(em the full documentation,
including the state machines.
